=begin pod

=head1 MCP::Server::Tool::Ask

An ask-the-user tool for L<MCP::Server|https://github.com/m-doughty/MCP-Server>,
built on genuine server-side elicitation.

Every other tool a model can call is something it does I<to> the world. This
is the one that goes the other way: the model has run out of information, and
the person who asked for the work is the only one who has it. The pack
registers a single tool — C<ask> — which renders a form, blocks until the user
fills it in, and hands the answers back to the model as JSON.

Underneath it is the 2026-07-28 multi round-trip request pattern: the tool call
is answered with C<resultType: "input_required">, the client shows the user a
form, and the call resumes exactly where it stopped. Nothing is polled, nothing
is faked client-side, and the handler is never re-run.

=head2 Synopsis

Plug it into a server you are building by hand:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use MCP::Server;
use MCP::Server::Tool::Ask;

my $server = MCP::Server.new(:name<assistant>, :version<1.0>);

$server.plug: MCP::Server::Tool::Ask.new;   # registers user_ask

$server.run;   # stdin/stdout
=end code

Or name it in a one-shot server, with no C<use> statement at all — the toolkit
is loaded at runtime:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use MCP::Server;

MCP::Server.new(
    :name<assistant>,
    :tools['Ask' => { timeout => 120 }],
).run;
=end code

Or hand C<raku-mcp> a config file:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
  "name": "assistant",
  "version": "1.0",
  "instructions": "Ask before guessing.",
  "tools": {
    "Ask": { "timeout": 120 },
    "FileSystem": { "root": "/srv/notes" }
  }
}
=end code

=begin code :lang<shell>
raku-mcp --config=assistant.json

# Or without a file:
raku-mcp --tool='Ask={"timeout":120}'
=end code

=head2 Configuration

=begin table
Key     | Type | Default | Meaning
======= | ==== | ======= | =======
timeout | Real | unset   | Seconds to wait for one answer before treating it as a cancellation. Unset means the wait is bounded only by the server's own C<elicitation-ttl> (five minutes by default). Zero or negative dies.
=end table

C<prefix> is reserved by the framework rather than the pack: pass it to
C<.plug> as C<< :prefix<...> >>, or put it beside the toolkit's settings in a
C<:tools> entry or config file. Without one, the pack's default prefix C<user>
applies, so the tool is C<user_ask>; an explicit empty prefix registers it
bare.

=head2 What the client has to support

The tool needs somebody to ask. That means one of:

=item A B<2026-07-28 client that declared the elicitation capability>
(C<< elicitation: { form: {} } >> in its C<_meta> C<clientCapabilities>). This
is the real path: the question goes to the client, the client asks the human.
=item A server configured with B<C<:&on-elicit>>, which answers questions
locally. That covers legacy-era clients, the LLM tool bridge
(C<execute-tool-calls>), and anything driving handlers directly.

With neither, calling the tool comes back as an error result naming which of
the two is missing — it does not hang, and it does not invent an answer. See
the "Elicitation" section of C<MCP::Server>'s documentation for the whole
picture.

=head2 The tool

=head3 ask(message, questions?)

=begin table
Parameter | Type   | Required | Meaning
========= | ====== | ======== | =======
message   | string | yes      | The framing: why the model is asking. Shown above the form.
questions | array  | no       | The fields to ask for. Omit it (or send an empty list) to ask one free-form question.
=end table

Each entry of C<questions> is an object:

=begin table
Key      | Type    | Default  | Meaning
======== | ======= | ======== | =======
name     | string  | required | The key the answer comes back under. Must be unique within the call.
prompt   | string  | —        | What to show the user for this field. Becomes the property's C<description>.
type     | string  | "string" | One of C<string>, C<number>, C<boolean>.
options  | array   | —        | Strings to choose between; becomes an C<enum>. Only valid on a string question.
required | boolean | true     | Whether the user must fill this field in.
=end table

A call with three fields:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
  "message": "I need a few details before I can file the ticket.",
  "questions": [
    { "name": "component", "prompt": "Which component?", "options": ["api", "ui", "docs"] },
    { "name": "severity",  "prompt": "How bad is it?",   "type": "number" },
    { "name": "notes",     "prompt": "Anything else?",   "required": false }
  ]
}
=end code

...becomes this C<requestedSchema> on the wire:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "component": { "type": "string", "description": "Which component?",
                   "enum": ["api", "ui", "docs"] },
    "severity":  { "type": "number", "description": "How bad is it?" },
    "notes":     { "type": "string", "description": "Anything else?" }
  },
  "required": ["component", "severity"]
}
=end code

...and comes back to the model as text:

=begin code
{"component":"api","notes":"","severity":2}
=end code

Keys are sorted and the JSON is not pretty-printed: this text goes into a
model's context, where stability is worth more than insertion order and
newlines are worth nothing at all.

With no C<questions>, the form has a single required string field called
C<answer>, which is what a model reaching for "just ask them" wants nine times
out of ten:

=begin code
{"answer":"go ahead, but stage it first"}
=end code

=head2 Refusal is an answer

A user who declines or cancels gets a plain, non-error result:

=item C<The user declined to answer.>
=item C<The user cancelled the request.>

Neither carries C<isError>, and that is deliberate. C<isError> tells a model
that something went wrong and that trying again might work — so an error result
here would send it straight back to ask the same person the same question. A
person saying no is the system working. The tool's description tells the model
as much: carry on without the answer, or stop and explain why it cannot.

The same goes for a client that answers with something that is not an
C<ElicitResult> at all: that is read as a decline rather than as a fault.

=head2 Errors

These I<are> error results, because each is a call the model can fix and
retry:

=item A missing, non-string or empty C<message>.
=item C<questions> that is not a list, or an entry that is not an object.
=item A question with no C<name>, or two questions sharing one — answers come
back keyed by name, so a duplicate would silently lose one.
=item An unsupported C<type>, C<options> on a non-string question, an empty
C<options> list, or a C<required> flag that is not a boolean.
=item An unknown key in a question object, listing the valid ones.

And, as above, calling the tool at all when there is nobody to ask.

=head2 Security and etiquette

=item B<This tool interrupts a human.> It is not free the way reading a file
is. A model that asks about everything is worse than one that asks about
nothing, and the tool description is written to push it toward asking only
when it genuinely cannot proceed. If you find a model over-using it, say so in
the server's C<instructions>.
=item B<An answer is user input, not truth.> It arrives as JSON straight from
whatever the client's form produced. Validate it before acting on it, exactly
as you would any other input, and do not treat "the user typed it" as
authorisation for something the user did not understand they were authorising.
=item B<A parked call holds server state.> Each unanswered question occupies a
place in the server's elicitation table (64 by default) until it is answered or
the TTL expires. Set C<timeout> if you would rather give up sooner than the
server-wide TTL.
=item B<Asking is inherently consented.> A permission layer in front of the
server — Claude Code's rules,
L<MCP::Client::Policy|https://github.com/m-doughty/MCP-Client> — should
normally allow C<user_ask> outright: prompting the user for permission to
prompt the user helps nobody.
=item B<This pack declares no MCP annotations, deliberately.> C<ask> writes
nothing, so C<< readOnlyHint => True >> would be defensible — and a host
reads that (with C<idempotentHint>) as permission to run two of them at once,
which is the last thing a tool that parks the call on a human wants: two
questions racing to the front of one person's attention is a worse
conversation than two questions in a row. It is not idempotent either. Asking
the same question twice asks a person twice. "Safe to describe as read-only"
and "safe to run beside its neighbours" are different questions about the same
tool, and an annotation answers the second.

=head2 Testing

=begin code :lang<shell>
prove6 -Ilib t/                          # with MCP::Server installed
prove6 -Ilib -I../MCP-Server/lib t/      # against a sibling checkout
=end code

C<t/02-tool.rakutest> drives every question shape through a server with a
scripted C<:on-elicit> behind it; C<t/03-mrtr.rakutest> does the same thing the
long way round, over a real multi round-trip with no local callback anywhere.

=head2 Examples

=item C<examples/ask-server.raku> — a Streamable HTTP server with a
terminal-prompt C<:on-elicit> fallback, so both paths can be watched side by
side: a client that declares the capability gets the form over the wire, and
one that does not sends the question to the terminal the server was started
from. (HTTP rather than stdio because a stdio server has already spent stdin
and stdout on the protocol.)

